package num

// Elementary functions that give the same bits on every machine.
//
// The C library's sin on Linux and its sin on macOS are different programs. They
// seldom disagree — a few times in a hundred thousand arguments — but seldom is
// enough: a long simulation meets one of those arguments, and from there it
// answers one thing on a laptop and another on a server. They can also change
// from one release of the library to the next. And a det function cannot call
// them at all, since they are outside the language.
//
// These are written with nothing but IEEE addition, subtraction, multiplication,
// division and square root, which every machine rounds the same way, and the
// compiler never fuses a multiply into an add behind them. So the bits are the
// same everywhere, and each function is within one unit in the last place of the
// true value.
//
// The polynomial kernels and the structure of Exp and Log are fdlibm's. The
// reduction of large arguments for Sin and Cos is exact integer arithmetic on the
// bits of 2/pi, written here. Sqrt is correctly rounded.
//
// ====================================================
// Copyright (C) 1993 by Sun Microsystems, Inc. All rights reserved.
